# Blaine Miller — the portfolio as a terminal app.      curl -sL wemiller.com/shell | sh
#
# Generated by scripts/build-site.mjs from scripts/tui.template.sh and /content.
# It installs nothing unless you ask it to (D, then y): it draws to your terminal and, when you
# press enter on something, opens that link in your browser. POSIX sh + awk + stty only.
#
#   up/down or j/k  move        left/right, l, tab or 1-5  switch section
#   enter or o      open        g / G  first / last     q  quit
#   h or ?          every control, in words (the legend shows only the keys after a moment)
#   t / T           time machine: pick a day or type a date / back to the present
#   s               seasonal effects on or off (WEMILLER_SEASONS=0)
#   /               search everything, enter jumps to the match
#   r               download the resume as a PDF into the folder you ran this from
#                   (Markdown instead if the download fails)
#   m               music: Tom Jr., a little Tom, composes synthwave, lo-fi and rock as it plays.
#                   It is written in awk right here and downloads nothing; the sound goes to the
#                   player your system already has (afplay, pw-play, paplay or aplay) through a
#                   temporary folder that it removes when you turn it off or quit.
#   - / +           music volume down / up (0-10; WEMILLER_VOLUME sets where it starts)
#   L               next language
#   D               install my dotfiles (wemiller.com/dotfiles) for the user running this. It asks
#                   first and does nothing unless you press y: it saves ~/.zshlocal (or
#                   ~/.ashlocal where there is no zsh or bash) and adds one line to your shell
#                   startup files so new shells load it.
#
# It speaks English, 简体中文, 日本語, Deutsch, Français, Español, 한국어, Português and Italiano:
# WEMILLER_LANG=ja (or any of zh, ja, de, fr, es, ko, pt, it, en) picks one, otherwise your locale
# does (LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES, LANG). A terminal that is not UTF-8 gets English in plain ASCII.
